From c3d513ba137aae64cd4cd34598bf437c3d0f8835 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 18:50:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix audio in in-app browsers by claiming the gesture up front
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Tapping a track ran an async resolve before reaching play(). Desktop Chrome
allows that because activation is sticky for the document, but WKWebView —
what Instagram, Facebook and Messenger embed on iOS — only honours a gesture
within the task that received it, so playback was refused.

- PlayerEngine.unlock() primes the <audio> element with 0.05s of silence and
  resumes the AudioContext. It is called synchronously at the top of playAt
  and toggle, before any await, plus once on the first touch anywhere.
- A blocked play() now reports which app is restricting it instead of a
  generic autoplay message, and clears the playing flag it wrongly set.
- InAppBrowserNotice points at Open in browser, and at the iOS ringer switch,
  which mutes HTML audio regardless of anything the page does.

The e2e suites cannot reproduce the original failure: Chromium grants sticky
document activation, so the pre-fix build passes the same test. They do
confirm no regression under the strictest policy Chromium offers.

Also stops the mobile blinder check sampling its baseline from one frame,
which intermittently landed on a strobe flash and failed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
